{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XP398Dk5mQrnNAAfaULMr3","type":"tool_result","content":"119:## Ch 9 — Every Pod Has an Address  [PLANNED]\n120-\n121-| § | Title | Owns |\n122-|---|---|---|\n123-| §1 | Four Rules and Who Wires Them | the Kubernetes network model's four requirements; Pod IP as cluster-routable; NAT-free pod-to-pod; **CNI** as the pluggable implementation and what a CNI plugin does. **Second of the four pluggable interfaces**; shared network namespace refers to Ch 5 §1 |\n124-| §2 | A Name That Outlives the Pod | the Service object; ClusterIP; the virtual IP; why controller-driven churn (Ch 6 §1) forces an indirection |\n125-| §3 | Four Types, Stacked | ClusterIP → NodePort → LoadBalancer as strictly additive layers; ExternalName as the outlier that resolves rather than routes |\n126-| §4 | Selecting the Backends | Service selector → EndpointSlice; Endpoints as the legacy form; **readiness gates endpoint membership** (probe definitions at Ch 5 §7); headless Services; Services without selectors and hand-written endpoints |\n127-| §5 | How the Traffic Actually Gets There | the service proxy concept; kube-proxy modes (iptables, IPVS, nftables; userspace as historical); why the VIP has no process behind it |\n128-| §6 | Finding It by Name | CoreDNS as the cluster DNS addon; Service A/AAAA/SRV records; Pod DNS records; FQDN shape; search domains and namespace-relative resolution |\n129-| §7 | A Stable Name Over Churn | chapter synthesis (Zenith) |\n130-\n131-*Published pointers honored: `Ch 9 §1 — CNI and pod networking`; `Ch 9 §4 — readiness and Service endpoint membership`. Both were emitted in shipped text before this skeleton existed and are immovable.*\n132-\n133----","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fc2dd0c4-2b35-4d12-b970-d0021c296299","uuid":"1c6ab242-61ba-478c-839e-133e255143ce","timestamp":"2026-08-25T05:52:21.672Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"119:## Ch 9 — Every Pod Has an Address  [PLANNED]\n120-\n121-| § | Title | Owns |\n122-|---|---|---|\n123-| §1 | Four Rules and Who Wires Them | the Kubernetes network model's four requirements; Pod IP as cluster-routable; NAT-free pod-to-pod; **CNI** as the pluggable implementation and what a CNI plugin does. **Second of the four pluggable interfaces**; shared network namespace refers to Ch 5 §1 |\n124-| §2 | A Name That Outlives the Pod | the Service object; ClusterIP; the virtual IP; why controller-driven churn (Ch 6 §1) forces an indirection |\n125-| §3 | Four Types, Stacked | ClusterIP → NodePort → LoadBalancer as strictly additive layers; ExternalName as the outlier that resolves rather than routes |\n126-| §4 | Selecting the Backends | Service selector → EndpointSlice; Endpoints as the legacy form; **readiness gates endpoint membership** (probe definitions at Ch 5 §7); headless Services; Services without selectors and hand-written endpoints |\n127-| §5 | How the Traffic Actually Gets There | the service proxy concept; kube-proxy modes (iptables, IPVS, nftables; userspace as historical); why the VIP has no process behind it |\n128-| §6 | Finding It by Name | CoreDNS as the cluster DNS addon; Service A/AAAA/SRV records; Pod DNS records; FQDN shape; search domains and namespace-relative resolution |\n129-| §7 | A Stable Name Over Churn | chapter synthesis (Zenith) |\n130-\n131-*Published pointers honored: `Ch 9 §1 — CNI and pod networking`; `Ch 9 §4 — readiness and Service endpoint membership`. Both were emitted in shipped text before this skeleton existed and are immovable.*\n132-\n133----","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
